WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.920 --> 00:00:05.860
It's time to thread up the machine
and get knitting. Before we get started.

2
00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:10.420
Be sure you're using a yarn that's
the appropriate weight for your machine.

3
00:00:10.450 --> 00:00:13.300
Do you need yarn on cones
for machine knitting?

4
00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:16.340
No.
All you need is to have the yarn

5
00:00:16.370 --> 00:00:21.360
wound in a way that it will
easily feed into the machine.

6
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:26.660
Often you can use a center pull
ball from a yarn manufacturer.

7
00:00:26.690 --> 00:00:30.540
Some knitters will use a ball winder
and rewind everything.

8
00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:34.680
Skins, Hanks, balls, everything.

9
00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.780
If your ball Winder creates a flat bottom

10
00:00:37.810 --> 00:00:42.300
ball, you can also use the yarn
from the outside of the ball.

11
00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.140
Do whatever works for you.

12
00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:47.860
You don't want to spend a whole
lot of time dealing with knots

13
00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.120
as you're knitting.

14
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.100
Let's thread up the machine.

15
00:00:55.130 --> 00:00:59.380
Working on the right side of the mast,
take the end of the working yarn

16
00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:04.980
and thread it through the loop
in the triangular yarn guide.

17
00:01:05.010 --> 00:01:09.260
You'll then pass it through
the mast tension dial.

18
00:01:09.290 --> 00:01:12.900
Be sure to place the yarn
between the discs.

19
00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.410
Notice that there's a post inside.

20
00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:19.700
Be sure that the yarn
passes under that post.

21
00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:22.850
Adjust the tension dial
to the middle setting.

22
00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.460
Aligning the two triangles

23
00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:27.900
for now. As you work with different yarns,

24
00:01:27.930 --> 00:01:34.210
you can adjust the flow of the yarn here
based on the thickness of the yarn.

25
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:36.820
This tension dial, along with the carriage

26
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:41.800
tension dial, impacts
the gauge of your knitting.

27
00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.210
Place the yarn in the front
loop of the front arm feeder.

28
00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:49.290
Reach up and grab the right

29
00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:54.560
take-up spring and pass
the yarn through the loop.

30
00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:56.340
Secure the yarn in the clip.

31
00:01:56.370 --> 00:02:01.000
I'm a mask to temporarily hold it.

32
00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:07.420
This is how your mast should look.

33
00:02:07.450 --> 00:02:10.380
We'll start with some plain knitting

34
00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:16.380
first, we'll need to get the yarn
on the needles and form some stitches.

35
00:02:16.410 --> 00:02:18.730
The Caston we'll be using is a little

36
00:02:18.760 --> 00:02:20.980
different from the manual,
and you'll see why

37
00:02:21.010 --> 00:02:22.880
in a minute.

38
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:25.980
Be sure your carriage is
set for plain knitting.

39
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:28.730
Now this is a term you're
going to become familiar with.

40
00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:31.730
So take a mental picture.

41
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:37.400
Tension dial 6-8,
depending on your yarn.

42
00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:43.040
Holding Cam lever to N.

43
00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.940
No Cam buttons engaged.

44
00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:49.420
Use the release lever.

45
00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:52.480
Make sure they're all out.

46
00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.920
Set the change knob to N.

47
00:03:04.120 --> 00:03:07.180
The carriage is on the right.

48
00:03:07.210 --> 00:03:09.200
Use the flat side of your needle.

49
00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:12.610
Pusher and pull out 40 needles.

50
00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:17.020
Notice that the number strip is
marked from the center of the bed.

51
00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:19.180
To put 40 needles into work.

52
00:03:19.210 --> 00:03:25.940
We'll pull out 20 needles on each
side of the imaginary zero.

53
00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:31.700
It's common practice to knit in the middle
of the needle bed, but it's not required.

54
00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:33.180
Most patterns are written

55
00:03:33.210 --> 00:03:38.600
with the assumption that you've positioned
the knitting in the middle of the bed.

56
00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:40.140
For my Caston.

57
00:03:40.170 --> 00:03:44.860
I'm going to put every other
needle into out of work position.

58
00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:49.340
Out of work position is the A
position or all the way back.

59
00:03:49.370 --> 00:03:55.060
The remaining needles can be in B or
D position somewhere in the middle.

60
00:03:55.090 --> 00:03:59.860
Remove the yarn from the yarn clip
and thread it in the yarn feeder.

61
00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:04.340
Wiggle it back and forth to make
sure that it flows evenly.

62
00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:06.780
Keep hold of the tail of yarn with your

63
00:04:06.810 --> 00:04:11.380
left hand and move
the carriage to the left.

64
00:04:11.410 --> 00:04:15.480
You will have loops on every other needle.

65
00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:25.300
I like to use a chip clip or a spring
type clothes pin to secure the yarn tail.

66
00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:30.440
This ensures that the end
stitches knit correctly.

67
00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:36.340
Next, you need to hang your cast on comb
in the loops that you just formed.

68
00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:39.300
If you don't use a cast on comb and you

69
00:04:39.330 --> 00:04:41.620
try to knit back,
you're going to get a mess.

70
00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:43.380
Trust me.

71
00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:49.580
Make sure the Caston comb is in each
one of the loops between the needles.

72
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.860
You can hang a claw weight
in the cast on comb.

73
00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:57.880
This is especially helpful
when Casting on larger pieces.

74
00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:02.700
Pull the out of work needles
into working position.

75
00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.800
Having a total of 40 needles in work.

76
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.340
Knit to the right.

77
00:05:09.360 --> 00:05:12.900
You've completed your cast on row.

78
00:05:12.920 --> 00:05:15.180
Now let's stop here for a minute.

79
00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:19.780
The manual tells you to work
the Cast on over 40 needles.

80
00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:25.020
I find that the Cast on comb teeth can be
bent really easy and they're really

81
00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:30.660
wobbly, and I find it easier
to start with every other needle.

82
00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:31.940
Either way, you work it

83
00:05:31.970 --> 00:05:36.140
this is a fast Cast on and it has its uses.

84
00:05:36.170 --> 00:05:41.740
But it is sloppy and it really wouldn't
be appropriate for most knitting.

85
00:05:41.770 --> 00:05:45.540
There are many more Caston
methods you can use.

86
00:05:45.570 --> 00:05:48.420
Knit it Now now has an entire classroom,

87
00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:54.700
course, and five day challenge
covering Caston and find off methods.

88
00:05:54.730 --> 00:06:00.460
You will discover and practice
your own preferred Cast  on method.

89
00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:03.220
For now, we want to just get some stitches

90
00:06:03.250 --> 00:06:08.000
on the machine and explore
the working of the machine.

91
00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:10.500
Now we can start knitting.

92
00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:13.620
The key to success is rhythm.

93
00:06:13.650 --> 00:06:16.260
It's tempting to pass that carriage back

94
00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:20.700
and forth really fast,
especially with small pieces like this.

95
00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:24.860
But consider what's happening
under the carriage.

96
00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:28.780
As you knit,
each needle is being pulled out

97
00:06:28.800 --> 00:06:30.180
in turn.

98
00:06:30.210 --> 00:06:33.820
The working yarn is placed in the hook
of the needle,

99
00:06:33.850 --> 00:06:39.460
and as the needle is pulled back,
the latch of the needle closes and it

100
00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:44.980
pulls that working yarn through the
previous stitch, forming a new stitch.

101
00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:51.280
And at the same time, the next needle is
being positioned to do the same thing.

102
00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:56.620
Knitting quickly can cause
an interruption to this process.

103
00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:58.420
You don't have to go slow,

104
00:06:58.450 --> 00:07:05.580
but an even speed is best give
the carriage time to work its magic.

105
00:07:05.600 --> 00:07:07.380
Another thing to consider.

106
00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:12.300
Do not take the carriage too far
past the end of the knitting.

107
00:07:12.330 --> 00:07:14.640
If you're getting loops on the edges as

108
00:07:14.670 --> 00:07:19.140
you knit, watch that the carriage passes
the edge of the knitting,

109
00:07:19.170 --> 00:07:25.020
but it doesn't pull too much yarn,
forming big old loops on the edges.

110
00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:32.480
You will develop your own rhythm and you
will develop a feel for the edges.

111
00:07:32.880 --> 00:07:35.780
Another thing to consider. As you knit,

112
00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:40.500
there's a lot going on. The yarn is
passing up through that tension mast.

113
00:07:40.530 --> 00:07:43.460
You're watching the carriage
pass back and forth.

114
00:07:43.480 --> 00:07:45.660
The row counter is clicking away.

115
00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:47.500
You're following a pattern.

116
00:07:47.530 --> 00:07:50.740
The knitting is growing before your eyes.

117
00:07:50.770 --> 00:07:55.420
By slowing down, you can easily
keep track of what's happening.

118
00:07:55.450 --> 00:07:58.620
You can look out for knots
in the working yarn.

119
00:07:58.650 --> 00:08:01.860
You can keep track of the number
of rows you're knitting.

120
00:08:01.890 --> 00:08:04.380
You can follow your pattern.

121
00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:08.020
I can't emphasize it enough. For success

122
00:08:08.040 --> 00:08:15.940
with your knitting (large piece or
small piece) knit with an even speed.

123
00:08:15.970 --> 00:08:19.500
Let's knit a few rows
to get a feel for things.

124
00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:24.220
As the knitting grows, you'll want
to hang claw weights along the edge.

125
00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:26.660
Don't worry, they won't
damage your knitting.

126
00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:33.080
They will just help the machine
form the edge stitches correctly

127
00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:39.140
and you can move the claw weights
up as the knitting grows.

128
00:08:39.170 --> 00:08:40.740
For larger pieces,

129
00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:44.780
you'll want to hang claw weights
in the middle of the knitting. Again,

130
00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:47.260
think about how those
stitches are being formed.

131
00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:49.980
If the stitches are loose and sloppy.

132
00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:56.360
As the needle pulls back to form a stitch,
the hook and or the latch can catch

133
00:08:56.390 --> 00:09:05.060
strands, misform stitches and even
jam the carriage. So use weights.

134
00:09:05.080 --> 00:09:07.380
Let's look at the row counter.

135
00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:11.540
If you're a hand knitter,
counting rows may seam foreign to you.

136
00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:15.820
Hand knitters can just simply
measure the length of their knitting

137
00:09:15.840 --> 00:09:16.340
machine.

138
00:09:16.370 --> 00:09:21.340
Knitting add weights and we have the
knitting stretched across the needle bed.

139
00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:24.420
We can't just accurately measure length.

140
00:09:24.440 --> 00:09:31.400
We must count rows and knit the length
according to our row gauge.

141
00:09:31.960 --> 00:09:34.660
As we pass the carriage back and forth,

142
00:09:34.690 --> 00:09:39.080
the row counter Tripper
increments the row counter.

143
00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:42.940
How do we get the knitting
off the machine?

144
00:09:42.970 --> 00:09:47.020
Well, while you're practicing,
the best way is to just break that working

145
00:09:47.050 --> 00:09:52.700
yarn, pass the carriage across and let
the work drop from the machine.

146
00:09:52.730 --> 00:09:56.360
We'll cover binding off in another video

147
00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:58.020
before moving on.

148
00:09:58.050 --> 00:10:01.220
I suggest you cast on knit.

149
00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:04.300
Drop the work a few times.

150
00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:05.940
The more you practice,

151
00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:11.480
the more you'll build your knitting
confidence and make the next steps easier.

